Fillets: an OnSame end's cap starts at the opposite contact's crossing
with the prolonged edge itself (mitcad#343).

The case: a rounding of radius 0.5 along a thread's chamfer cone, ending
where its contact on the cone runs into the thread's root cylinder (the
face at the end). The contact's approximated curve lies about 2e-6 off the
cone and crosses the cylinder at a small angle, so its point on the
cylinder (IntersUpdateOnSame) lay 1.3e-6 below the cone's edge with the
cylinder, an exact circle; the cap, the intersection of the blend's
approximated surface with the cylinder, started there too. The blend is
tangent to the cone along its contact, so the cap leaves that edge
tangentially, and nearly tangent to the cylinder it rises from it slowly:
it crossed the circle 0.026 from the vertex, and BRepCheck rejected the
cylinder's wire as self-intersecting (the circle's tolerance is 1e-7).

- IntersUpdateOnSame: where the faces are not tangent along the prolonged
  edge (the tangent case already seeks the crossing with the edge) and the
  contact's point on the face at the end lies within the tolerance of that
  edge's curve (unbounded: the contact may cross it on its prolongation),
  the contact's crossing with the edge on the face is taken (Update with
  the edge's curve on the face, as in the tangent case).
- PerformOneCorner: the cap computed by ChFi3d_ComputeCurves in an OnSame
  end is made to start at that point, on the blend and on the face, where
  it ends off it by no more than ten times the tolerance
  (ChFi3d_CapFromPoint: the end poles of its curves moved there).

Upstream: new.

